 ## Generation Modes

+### Nullable properties and raw responses
+
+Optional nullable object properties use `Option<Option<T>>`: `None` omits the
+property, `Some(None)` sends JSON null, and `Some(Some(value))` sends a value.
+Deserialization preserves the same three states. Required nullable properties
+use `Option<T>`, always serialize, and reject a missing property.
+
+`client.raw()` exposes the same typed operation methods and request validation,
+returning `reqwest::Response` before parsing. Use it when status codes, headers
+(including `Retry-After`), or unrecognized response bodies affect policy. The
+generated request type's `parse_response` method remains available for typed
+response parsing after inspecting metadata.
+
+`--bidirectional-serde` derives both serde directions for schema models. This
+supports CLI JSON output, local request fixtures, and response persistence.
+
+JSON object models preserve additional properties in a flattened map. Omitting
+`additionalProperties` permits arbitrary JSON values, as do `true` and an empty
+schema. An explicit `false` rejects unknown properties; a schema constrains the
+map values to its generated type.
+Multipart form parts correspond to named schema properties; the flattened map
+does not create a form part named `additional_properties`.
+
 The positional `mode` argument selects what you generate.

 ```text
@@ -1235,3 +1258,18 @@ pub struct Widget {
 | `--only` | *(none)* | Include only specified operations |
 | `--exclude` | *(none)* | Exclude specified operations |
 | `--all-schemas` | `false` | Generate all schemas regardless of usage |
+
+
+Fixed-length `prefixItems` arrays are represented as Rust tuples, including
+nested header pairs. Serde enforces positional types and exact tuple lengths.
+Variable-length tuple schemas are rejected explicitly. Complex tuple items
+(object, enum, or union) must be named schema references.
+
+### Literal object properties
+
+JSON Schema `const` on object properties constrains serialization and
+deserialization independently of `default`. Generated serde helpers reject
+other values and missing required properties, without including received data
+in the error. Optional properties may be omitted. This also lets untagged
+object unions with identical fields select a variant by their literal field
+values instead of accepting the first structurally matching variant.
